session:pr773 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:745 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-11-06T18:36:12Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-06T18:36:12Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr773Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #745 covers the error/SILENT/timeout policy for SPARQL SERVICE federation (epic #741), building on #744's wiring (PR #771). Investigation found #744 had left a real gap, not just the documented 'always-hard-error' interim behavior: collect_service_nodes filtered out every SILENT SERVICE node before pre-resolution ever ran, so a SILENT SERVICE against a perfectly healthy endpoint always returned empty results -- SILENT was being treated as 'never even try' instead of 'try, and only fall back to empty on failure'. Fixed by collecting SILENT nodes too (tagged with their own SILENT flag) and only suppressing the resulting ExecError for a SILENT call's failed fetch -- a successful SILENT fetch now joins its real results exactly like a non-SILENT one. Reused rather than added new error variants: #744 had already added ExecError::ServiceFetchFailed specifically anticipating this issue's policy nuance, and ExecError::Timeout already existed from #372's cooperative-deadline work, so no new ExecError variant was needed despite the issue text's original ServiceRequestFailed suggestion (written before #744 existed). The query's configured --timeout now bounds the SERVICE HTTP fetch itself: one wall-clock deadline is computed once per request in sparql_endpoint::query and its remaining duration is passed both into the async pre-resolution phase (as each fetch's reqwest per-request timeout) and into the synchronous execute_with_base call that follows, so the two phases share one budget instead of each independently getting the full configured timeout -- closing the 'hung remote endpoint ignores --timeout' gap the plan doc called out." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:773 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr773Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr773 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr773Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "A fetch failure specifically caused by the shared deadline elapsing is distinguished from every other fetch failure via a new pub(crate) FetchFailure enum (Timeout vs Other(String)) inside sparql_endpoint::service_federation, rather than string-matching reqwest's error message -- Timeout maps to ExecError::Timeout (503, consistent with every other query timeout), Other maps to ExecError::ServiceFetchFailed (502, the remote endpoint's fault). Also: the same (endpoint, body) SERVICE call can appear more than once in one query (e.g. both branches of a UNION) with different SILENT flags on each occurrence; these are deduplicated by ServiceKey before fetching (so each distinct call is only ever fetched once) and a pair's SILENT-ness for error-handling purposes is the logical AND across all its occurrences -- if even one occurrence in the query was non-SILENT, a failure on that shared fetch still propagates as an error rather than silently vanishing for the non-SILENT occurrence too." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered string-matching reqwest::Error's Display output (checking for 'timed out') to distinguish the deadline case the way sparql_endpoint::query used to distinguish Deadline::check's own Timeout message before #460/ExecError existed -- rejected as reintroducing exactly the brittleness #460 moved away from, when reqwest::Error::is_timeout() already gives a typed answer. Considered treating each duplicate (endpoint, body, silent) occurrence as an independently-fetched call (no dedup) -- rejected as both wasteful (N identical HTTP requests for one logical SERVICE call) and semantically murky once SILENT flags could disagree across occurrences of the same key." .
